</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Define cost function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(E_{i})=(1-||\hat{\mathbf{W}_{i}}||_{1}/\sum_{k=1}^{K}||\hat{\mathbf{W_{k}}}||_{1})\cdot\phi(E_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>𝐤</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(E_{i})=(1-||\hat{\mathbf{W}_{i}}||_{1}/\sum_{k=1}^{K}||\hat{\mathbf{W_{k}}}||_{1})\cdot\phi(E_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="||\cdot||_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\cdot||_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> means pixel-wise sum of the weight map, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the computational cost of an expert, here measured with FLOPS (Floating Point Operations).
We begin by constructing an expert queue based on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(E_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(E_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\{E_{1},\dots,E_{k},\dots,E_{\mathcal{K}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\{E_{1},\dots,E_{k},\dots,E_{\mathcal{K}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Phi(E_{1})\leq\Phi(E_{2})\leq\dots\leq\Phi(E_{\mathcal{K}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Phi(E_{1})\leq\Phi(E_{2})\leq\dots\leq\Phi(E_{\mathcal{K}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">notably, here <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> equals to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> defined above, we adopt a different notation to represent the reordering process used to construct the queue.
Intuitively, if experts have similar weights, we prioritize those with higher computational efficiency; if one has a dominant weight, we prioritize even if it has lower efficiency.
As shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In III-A Lazy Gating Mechanism ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> <span id="S3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#001272;">A</span>, the lazy gating algorithm processes experts sequentially, iterating from the front of the queue to the rear. During each iteration, the next expert is dequeued and queried to provide a traversability prediction. The internal processing of each expert is shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In III-A Lazy Gating Mechanism ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> <span id="S3.SS1.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#001272;">B</span>: At iteration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm maintains a prior traversability map <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which aggregates the predictions from the previous <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> experts. The <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th expert predicts a traversability map <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> while the gating network estimates the current expert’s confidence as weight <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The current expert’s output <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is then fused into the overall map:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}=\left.\left(\mathcal{T}_{k-1}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}+\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{k}\right)\right/\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}=\left.\left(\mathcal{T}_{k-1}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}+\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{k}\right)\right/\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the updated traversability map <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is then used as the new prior for the next iteration. The planner will take <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as input to compute the path <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and path cost <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\tau_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\tau_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> using <a href="#S2.E4" title="In Lower Level Router: Terrain Specialization ‣ II Hierarchical and Modular Mixture-of-Experts ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Given <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\tau_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\tau_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> as prior, the expected cost of the final path <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\tau_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\tau_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> will be constrained to lie within a value interval after any admissible update from expert <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> to expert <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>; we discuss how to get the value bounds in the following proposition. To reduce the computation overhead, we prune the rest of the experts if they do not significantly change the traversability output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition (Convergence Bound on Path Cost Under Incremental Traversability Updates).</span>
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the traversability map after <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> aggregative updates following <a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-A Lazy Gating Mechanism ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathcal{T}_{k})\doteq C(\tau^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathcal{T}_{k})\doteq C(\tau^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the path cost computed using <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define upper and lower expectation maps based on the remaining uncertainty in traversability (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) from step <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S3.E9.1" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9.1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left.\left(\mathcal{T}_{k}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}+0\cdot\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\right)\right/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left.\left(\mathcal{T}_{k}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}+0\cdot\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\right)\right/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9.1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{T}_{k}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\left/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\right.," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{T}_{k}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\left/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\right.,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E9.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}=\left.\left(\mathcal{T}_{k}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}+1\cdot\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\right)\right/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}=\left.\left(\mathcal{T}_{k}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{k}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}+1\cdot\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}\right)\right/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{W}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weight related to the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th update predicted by the routers, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of updates.
Intuitively, the lower expectation map assumes that all remaining (unexecuted) experts label the environment as untraversable, while the upper expectation map assumes they label it as fully traversable.
Then, the deviation of the current path cost from the final one is bounded as</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\left|C(\mathcal{T}_{k})-C(\mathcal{T}_{K})\right|\leq\delta_{k},\hskip 9.24994pt\text{where}\hskip 9.24994pt\delta_{k}:=C\left(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}\right)-C\left(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.097em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">where</mtext><mo lspace="0.930em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\left|C(\mathcal{T}_{k})-C(\mathcal{T}_{K})\right|\leq\delta_{k},\hskip 9.24994pt\text{where}\hskip 9.24994pt\delta_{k}:=C\left(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}\right)-C\left(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and the bound <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}\geq\delta_{k+1}\quad\forall k\in\{1,\ldots,K-1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}\geq\delta_{k+1}\quad\forall k\in\{1,\ldots,K-1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The full proof is provided in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition implies that the path cost converges within a provably shrinking bound as more expert updates are aggregated.
It guarantees that the path cost computed from a partially updated traversability map, obtained from only a subset of experts, deviates from the final cost computed using all experts by a bounded, computable margin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition establishes that activating all experts is unnecessary to obtain a satisfactory path solution, which provides theoretical justification for our lazy gating approach. Specifically, if the convergence bound <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> falls below a predefined threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the path cost is considered sufficiently stable, and the influence of remaining expert predictions is deemed negligible. This enables early termination of the update loop. This improves computational efficiency without significantly compromising path quality, as the traversability map has already converged within an acceptable tolerance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of Convergence Bound</span></h3>

<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12747v1/appendix-map2graph.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/78;" width="238" height="78" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><math id="S3.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> is modeled as an undirected graph <math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>. A path through the environment is modeled as a sequence of vertices <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=[v_{1},v_{2},\cdots,v_{t},\cdots,v_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=[v_{1},v_{2},\cdots,v_{t},\cdots,v_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math> connected via <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To support the proof, we model a traversability map <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as an undirected graph <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, where each vertex <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a pixel in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that is assigned a scalar traversability value (as shown in <a href="#S3.F4" title="In III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
An edge <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i},v_{j})\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i},v_{j})\in E</annotation></semantics></math> exists if the pixels are adjacent, capturing local connectivity. A path through the environment is defined as a sequence of vertices <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=[v_{1},v_{2},\cdots,v_{t},\cdots,v_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=[v_{1},v_{2},\cdots,v_{t},\cdots,v_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math> connected via <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The path planner seeks an optimal path <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing an objective function subject to feasibility constraints.
We formulate the path planning problem within graph <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as a constrained optimization problem <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}):" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}):</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(\mathcal{T})=\min\{f(\tau;\mathcal{T}):\tau\in S(\mathcal{T})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(\mathcal{T})=\min\{f(\tau;\mathcal{T}):\tau\in S(\mathcal{T})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rm{\displaystyle s.t.}\quad f(\tau;\mathcal{T})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}(1-\mathcal{T}(v_{t}))^{2}+f^{\prime}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rm{\displaystyle s.t.}\quad f(\tau;\mathcal{T})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}(1-\mathcal{T}(v_{t}))^{2}+f^{\prime}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E12Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S(\mathcal{T})=\{\tau|\mathcal{T}(v_{t})\geq\theta\ \forall t,v_{1}=v_{\rm src}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>src</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S(\mathcal{T})=\{\tau|\mathcal{T}(v_{t})\geq\theta\ \forall t,v_{1}=v_{\rm src}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau;\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau;\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> is the cost of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> under traversability map <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of feasible paths.
The first term in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> penalizes low-traversability paths, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates auxiliary objectives such as path length, smoothness, or dynamics constraints.
The definition of the feasible path set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the traversability of every vertex in the path should be at least of threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We proceed to prove the proposition in two parts. First, we establish that the bound <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined. Then, we show that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> monotonically decreases with increasing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>Validity and Bound Definition.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the definitions of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.E9.1" title="In Equation 9 ‣ III-A Lazy Gating Mechanism ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9a</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E9.2" title="Equation 9b ‣ III-A Lazy Gating Mechanism ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9b</span></a>, it follows that for any vertex <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> along a path <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}(v_{t})\leq\mathcal{T}_{k}(v_{t})\leq\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}(v_{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}(v_{t})\leq\mathcal{T}_{k}(v_{t})\leq\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}(v_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The elementwise inequality in <a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-B1 Validity and Bound Definition. ‣ III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> implies that, for any fixed path <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the traversability component of the cost function in <a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> is bounded:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau;\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq f(\tau;\mathcal{T}_{k})\leq f(\tau;\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau;\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq f(\tau;\mathcal{T}_{k})\leq f(\tau;\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the inequality in <a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-B1 Validity and Bound Definition. ‣ III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, it follows that
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\subseteq S(\mathcal{T}_{k})\subseteq S(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\subseteq S(\mathcal{T}_{k})\subseteq S(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The nesting of these feasible sets, combined with the elementwise cost bounds in <a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-B1 Validity and Bound Definition. ‣ III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, implies that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is a relaxation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a further relaxation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From the definition of problem relaxation, we obtain a corresponding ordering of the optimal path costs:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq C(\mathcal{T}_{k})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq C(\mathcal{T}_{k})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To extend this to the true final <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe that the elementwise value of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\small\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}-\mathcal{T}_{K}=-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\mathcal{T}_{j}\cdot\mathbf{W}_{j}\left/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}\right.\leq 0\Rightarrow\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}\leq\mathcal{T}_{K}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}-\mathcal{T}_{K}=-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\mathcal{T}_{j}\cdot\mathbf{W}_{j}\left/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}\right.\leq 0\Rightarrow\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}\leq\mathcal{T}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\small\mathcal{T}_{K}-\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}=\left.-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}(1-\mathcal{T}_{j})\cdot\mathbf{W}_{j}\right/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}\leq 0\Rightarrow\mathcal{T}_{K}\leq\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\mathcal{T}_{K}-\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}=\left.-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}(1-\mathcal{T}_{j})\cdot\mathbf{W}_{j}\right/\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}\leq 0\Rightarrow\mathcal{T}_{K}\leq\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, applying the same logic as above, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq C(\mathcal{T}_{K})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq C(\mathcal{T}_{K})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Combining <a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-B1 Validity and Bound Definition. ‣ III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-B1 Validity and Bound Definition. ‣ III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>, we conclude:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C(\mathcal{T}_{k})-\mathcal{T}_{K}|\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C(\mathcal{T}_{k})-\mathcal{T}_{K}|\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This establishes that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}=C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}=C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a valid upper bound on the absolute error between the current and final cost estimates as stated in <a href="#S3.E10" title="In III-A Lazy Gating Mechanism ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>Monotonicity of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that the convergence bound <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}=C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}=C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing over iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we examine the difference between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}-\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}=\left(\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k+1}-1\right)\cdot\frac{\mathbf{W}_{k+1}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}}\leq 0\Rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}\leq\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}-\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}=\left(\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k+1}-1\right)\cdot\frac{\mathbf{W}_{k+1}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}}\leq 0\Rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}\leq\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for the pessimistic estimate:</p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\small\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}-\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}=-\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k+1}\cdot\frac{\mathbf{W}_{k+1}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}}\leq 0\Rightarrow\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}\geq\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}-\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}=-\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{k+1}\cdot\frac{\mathbf{W}_{k+1}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathbf{W}_{j}}\leq 0\Rightarrow\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}}\geq\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, applying the same logic as in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="III-B1 Validity and Bound Definition. ‣ III-B Proof of Convergence Bound ‣ III Lazy Gating Mechanism for Expert Pruning ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B1</span></a>, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})\leq C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the convergence bound satisfies:</p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k+1}=C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})=\delta_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k+1}=C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k+1}})\leq C(\underline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})-C(\overline{\mathcal{T}_{k}})=\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This establishes that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is monotonically non-increasing with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Training Strategy</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first pretrain the gating networks on large-scale partially-aligned semantic segmentation datasets using human-prior-based label mappings to encourage generalization. Then, we fine-tune the router and experts end-to-end on small-scale multi-modal data with weak supervision derived from expert-label consistency.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pre-Training on Large-Scale Partially-Aligned Data</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Classes</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Trav. Cost</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ass. Exp.</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">sidewalk, floor, crosswalk, bike lane, etc</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S4.T1.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{free}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{free}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">M</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">gravel, sand, snow, low grass, etc</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T1.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.3.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{mid1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{mid1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">NN</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">road, parking area</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T1.3.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.3.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{mid2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{mid2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">NN</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">terrain (tall grass, bush, dirt)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T1.3.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.3.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{mid3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{mid3}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">NN</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">person, curb, wall, other obstacles</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T1.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.3.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{obs}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">M</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Semantics to assigned expert. M: model-based method; NN: Neural Networks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gating networks are first pretrained on large-scale data that contains various datasets with a wide domain distribution to learn the broad and general features.
These data include 32,000 images from three single-sensory datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, with each dataset corresponding to a distinct domain.
However, it is impractical to directly use these datasets for training, as they contain only 2D images without synchronized depth information. This limitation makes it difficult to implement certain experts, such as model-based approaches that rely on geometric data.
We solve this problem by mapping semantic or instance segmentation labels to expert selection labels based on human knowledge.
The label mapping encourages model-based approaches to handle terrains with clear geometric features, while assigning terrains where semantic information is more critical to traversability analysis to various neural networks.
An example of the label mapping is shown in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In IV-A Pre-Training on Large-Scale Partially-Aligned Data ‣ IV Training Strategy ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.
We encourage the gating networks to assign higher confidence to model-based experts with terrains such as roads or sidewalks. Meanwhile, higher confidence is encouraged to be assigned to experts based on neural networks on terrains such as grass or water surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although human priors do not perfectly capture the performance of each expert across domains or terrains, our experiments demonstrate that this pre-training strategy effectively paves the way for the subsequent fine-tuning stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">End-to-End Fine-Tunning for Task-Specific Optimization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Leveraging human priors eliminates the need for expensive multi-sensory data but introduces bias from human priors. Therefore, in the fine-tuning process, we train the model with a weakly supervised method on small-scale multi-modality data. We use 3,000 multi-sensory samples from both indoor and outdoor environments, including structured datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and unstructured outdoor data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, all featuring 2D images paired with synchronized depth maps as inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the set of expert traversability predictions <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground-truth traversability map <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate a routing supervision label <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math> by selecting, at each pixel, the prediction from the expert whose output most closely matches the ground truth.
Specifically, for each expert <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the pixel-wise squared error at location <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{k}(i,j)=\left(\mathcal{T}_{k}(i,j)-\mathcal{T}_{GT}(i,j)\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{k}(i,j)=\left(\mathcal{T}_{k}(i,j)-\mathcal{T}_{GT}(i,j)\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The routing supervising label at pixel <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is then determined by selecting the expert with the minimum error:</p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\mathcal{W}_{\text{sup}}(i,j)=\mathcal{T}_{k^{*}}(i,j),\hskip 9.24994pt\text{where}\hskip 9.24994ptk^{*}=\mathop{\arg\min}_{k\in\{1,2,\dots,K\}}\varepsilon_{k}(i,j)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒲</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">sup</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.097em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">where</mtext><mo lspace="0.930em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ε</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\mathcal{W}_{\text{sup}}(i,j)=\mathcal{T}_{k^{*}}(i,j),\hskip 9.24994pt\text{where}\hskip 9.24994ptk^{*}=\mathop{\arg\min}_{k\in\{1,2,\dots,K\}}\varepsilon_{k}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this procedure across all pixel locations constructs the full supervision map for training the router.
Guided by the router label <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we unfreeze and jointly fine-tune both the routers and experts to promote better alignment and co-adaptation.
The domain router is trained to prioritize the expert whose prediction best matches the ground truth at each input, while each domain expert is fine-tuned to improve its performance on the terrain regions where it is selected as the best predictor.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiment Results</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the cross-domain traversability estimation ability and computational efficiency of <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span>, we compare our approach with 4 state-of-the-art baselines that can run in real-time: Falco <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, Mask2Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> for indoor, MaskFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> for structured outdoor, and GANav <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> for unstructured outdoor environment. Additionally, we include four ablation studies, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> without fine-tuning and lazy gating (Ours w/o FT/LG), without lazy gating (Ours w/o LG), without lower-level lazy gating (Ours w/o LLG) and without fine-tuning (Ours w/o FT), to evaluate the individual contributions of these components to overall performance.
Scene overview for all three domains are shown in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.A. The indoor experiment is conducted in real world, outdoor experiments use test sequences from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate performance using four metrics: <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Primitives Quality: </span> We introduce aligned-path traversability estimation mean square error (<math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>) to measure the mean squared error between estimated and ground-truth traversability along candidate motion primitives, which directly reflects planning quality with arbitrary goals. It can be formulated as: <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{P}=\left\lVert S(\mathcal{T})-S(\mathcal{T}_{gt})\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\big/\left|S(\mathcal{T}_{gt})\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{P}=\left\lVert S(\mathcal{T})-S(\mathcal{T}_{gt})\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\big/\left|S(\mathcal{T}_{gt})\right|</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Traversability Map Quality: </span> We introduce traversability map estimation mean square error (<math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>) to assess the accuracy of the predicted traversability map;
<span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Efficiency: </span> is quantified in GFLOPS; and
<span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalized path quality (<math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Given any path <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, goal <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{goal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{goal}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the path quality as <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(\tau)=1-f(\tau;\mathcal{T}_{gt},p_{goal})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(\tau)=1-f(\tau;\mathcal{T}_{gt},p_{goal})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau;\mathcal{T},p_{goal})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau;\mathcal{T},p_{goal})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the path cost (as defined in <a href="#S2.E4" title="In Lower Level Router: Terrain Specialization ‣ II Hierarchical and Modular Mixture-of-Experts ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Path quality ratio <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is then formulated as: <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}=\eta(\tau_{est})/\eta(\tau_{gt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}=\eta(\tau_{est})/\eta(\tau_{gt})</annotation></semantics></math>. This metric ranges between 0 and 1, with higher values indicating better path quality.
Since our approach involves the lazy gating mechanism that doesn’t need to produce a full traversability map for path planning, we don’t record <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> for our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We report GFLOPs instead
of runtime because some experts in our MoE framework are implemented in C++
while others in Python. Thus, we believe GFLOPs provide a fairer metric for
comparison. We here
report the average runtimes estimated in outdoor scenario to demonstrate our time efficiency: Gating network 6.0ms;
Model-based expert 6.2ms; Learning-based expert 64.3ms; and path calculation
119.8ms, and the average processing time is 163.8ms. We believe this is good for
common Navigation, although the code is not optimized yet.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12747v1/Experiment.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/326;" width="452" height="326" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Sample frames from three domains.<span id="S5.F5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
</span>(A)<span id="S5.F5.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Overview of the test scenes.
</span>(B)<span id="S5.F5.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Representative frame with results: a semi-transparent traversability map is overlaid on the RGB image, red-to-blue means non-to-fully traversable; candidate motion primitives are drawn on the map and colored by their average traversability from red to gold; the green curve marks the final path.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative Results</span>
As illustrated in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#001272;">.B</span>, our method navigates complex environments, such as <a href="#S5.F5" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#001272;">.B<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#000000" height="13.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-2.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 13.74 13.74" width="13.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,13.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(6.87,0) translate(0,6.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.59 0 C 6.59 3.64 3.64 6.59 0 6.59 C -3.64 6.59 -6.59 3.64 -6.59 0 C -6.59 -3.64 -3.64 -6.59 0 -6.59 C 3.64 -6.59 6.59 -3.64 6.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span>, safely by primarily leveraging model-based experts while querying learning-based experts only when semantic cues are critical, achieving robust performance across all domains and terrain types. In <a href="#S5.F5" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#001272;">.B<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p4.pic2" class="ltx_picture" color="#000000" height="13.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-2.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 13.74 13.74" width="13.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,13.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(6.87,0) translate(0,6.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.59 0 C 6.59 3.64 3.64 6.59 0 6.59 C -3.64 6.59 -6.59 3.64 -6.59 0 C -6.59 -3.64 -3.64 -6.59 0 -6.59 C 3.64 -6.59 6.59 -3.64 6.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.p4.pic2.1" class="ltx_text">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span>, learning-based expert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> fail to strictly adhere to safety constraints, potentially causing the robot falling off a curb into traffic, while <span id="S5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> utilizes geometric perception from model-based methods to maintain safety. Conversely, in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S5.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#001272;">.B<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p4.pic3" class="ltx_picture" color="#000000" height="13.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-2.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 13.74 13.74" width="13.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,13.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(6.87,0) translate(0,6.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.59 0 C 6.59 3.64 3.64 6.59 0 6.59 C -3.64 6.59 -6.59 3.64 -6.59 0 C -6.59 -3.64 -3.64 -6.59 0 -6.59 C 3.64 -6.59 6.59 -3.64 6.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.p4.pic3.1" class="ltx_text">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span> and <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p4.pic4" class="ltx_picture" color="#000000" height="13.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-2.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 13.74 13.74" width="13.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,13.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(6.87,0) translate(0,6.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.59 0 C 6.59 3.64 3.64 6.59 0 6.59 C -3.64 6.59 -6.59 3.64 -6.59 0 C -6.59 -3.64 -3.64 -6.59 0 -6.59 C 3.64 -6.59 6.59 -3.64 6.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.p4.pic4.1" class="ltx_text">4</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>, the learning-based experts’ semantic perception correctly identifies non- or low-traversable areas (e.g., stop sign, grass), whereas the model-based method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> mistakenly classify grass as traversable due to its geometric similarity to planar surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Domain Traversability Estimation Accuracy.</span>
As shown in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, <span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> is the only method that can generalize well across all three domains, achieving best or second best performance among <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In structured outdoor environment where learning-based expert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and Model-based one <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> have complementary expertise, the advantage of <span id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> becomes significant and we achieve the best performance among <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In indoor environment, our approach achieves comparable accuracy compared to domain-specific baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> with only a <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.03</annotation></semantics></math> drop in <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same phenomenon extends to unstructured outdoor environment with a <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> drop in <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This trend aligns with findings reported in other MoE works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, which indicates that MoE methods tend to deliver similar or occasionally slightly lower performance in domains where a single expert already excels. Additionally, our results are impacted by hardware misalignment and synchronization issues between the RGB camera and lidar or depth camera, which can be ovserved in Model-based method part in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
<span id="S5.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness of FT: </span>
Comparing <span id="S5.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> w/o FT/LG to <span id="S5.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> w/o LG, fine-tuning improves estimation accuracy up to <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41\%</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>54</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54\%</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. When lazy gating is applied (Ours w/o FT v.s. Ours in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>), fine-tuning balances <math id="S5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and GFLOPS. Specifically, in the indoor scenario, fine-tuning shifts weights toward model-based methods, improving efficiency with small <math id="S5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> loss. Conversely, in outdoor environments, fine-tuning allocates more weights to learning-based methods, enhancing <math id="S5.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency and Effectiveness for Path Planning.</span>
Across all domains, <span id="S5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> has substantially higher efficiency than the best performing expert, marked in blue in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, by up to <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>76</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76\%</annotation></semantics></math> With the guarantee solution quality introduced by the lazy gating mechanism,
The result <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are within <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.03</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <span id="S5.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> w/o LG, which is within the bound set as <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S5.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness of LG: </span>
Comparing <span id="S5.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> to our method w/o LG, lazy gating can decrease the computational overhead up to <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with only up to <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math> loss in <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Interestingly, we found the upper level lazy router achieves over <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence and over <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="98\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>98</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">98\%</annotation></semantics></math> in accuracy across all domains, meaning that keeping the domain lazy router will not affect the estimation accuracy but significantly improve the computational efficiency, shown in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In V Experiment Results ‣ NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>: Ours w/o LG and Ours w/o LLG. In this case, our method can still improve the computational efficiency by up to <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby reducing the average inference time to 164 milliseconds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, domain-specific approaches show satisfying performance within their area of expertise, they exhibit significantly weaker performance in out-of-distribution scenarios. <span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NavMoE</span> demonstrates more robust and effective corss-domain traversability estimation accuracy.
This reinforces our intuition that a robust and effective navigation model can be achieved by hierarchically combining experts with different expertise, paired with an efficient routing mechanism.
Furthermore, our domain-gating mechanism allows the model to seamlessly scale with new learning-based navigation models across any domain, requiring only minimal fine-tuning of the gating networks.</p>
</div>
